### Why does a circuit breaker exist?

When a downstream service is slow or down, naive callers keep sending requests that each wait for a timeout, consuming threads until the caller itself fails. This is how one failure cascades into a system-wide outage.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class CascadeWithoutBreaker {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Without circuit breaker:");
        System.out.println("  Credit-score service is down");
        System.out.println("  Each call waits for 30s timeout");
        System.out.println("  200 threads all waiting -> thread pool exhausted");
        System.out.println("  Loan service cannot serve ANY request -> cascade failure");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("With circuit breaker:");
        System.out.println("  After 10 failures, circuit opens");
        System.out.println("  Calls fail fast in 1ms with fallback");
        System.out.println("  Threads freed instantly -> loan service keeps working");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Without circuit breaker:
  Credit-score service is down
  Each call waits for 30s timeout
  200 threads all waiting -> thread pool exhausted
  Loan service cannot serve ANY request -> cascade failure

With circuit breaker:
  After 10 failures, circuit opens
  Calls fail fast in 1ms with fallback
  Threads freed instantly -> loan service keeps working
```

### How does Resilience4j implement the three states?

CLOSED: calls pass through, outcomes recorded in a sliding window. OPEN: calls rejected immediately. HALF_OPEN: a few trial calls test if the dependency recovered.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class CreditScoreClient {

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "creditScore", fallbackMethod = "fallbackScore")
    public int getCreditScore(String accountId) {
        // When failure rate > 50%, circuit opens
        // Calls are rejected with CallNotPermittedException
        return externalCreditService.getScore(accountId);
    }

    public int fallbackScore(String accountId, Throwable t) {
        System.out.println("Circuit breaker active for " + accountId
                + ", reason: " + t.getClass().getSimpleName());
        return 650; // Safe default score
    }
}
```

```yaml
resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    instances:
      creditScore:
        slidingWindowType: COUNT_BASED
        slidingWindowSize: 10
        failureRateThreshold: 50
        waitDurationInOpenState: 30s
        permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState: 3
        slowCallRateThreshold: 80
        slowCallDurationThreshold: 3s
```

```text
State transitions:
  CLOSED: failure rate 30% (< 50%) -> stays CLOSED
  CLOSED: failure rate 60% (> 50%) -> transitions to OPEN
  OPEN: 30 seconds pass -> transitions to HALF_OPEN
  HALF_OPEN: 3 trial calls succeed -> transitions to CLOSED
  HALF_OPEN: 2 of 3 trial calls fail -> transitions back to OPEN
```

### How does Retry differ from Circuit Breaker?

Retry re-attempts a failed call. It is for transient glitches. Circuit Breaker stops calling entirely when a dependency is clearly down.

| Aspect | Retry | Circuit Breaker |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Handle transient failures | Prevent cascade from sustained failure |
| Behavior | Re-attempt the same call | Stop making calls entirely |
| Duration | Seconds (backoff between attempts) | Minutes (wait in open state) |
| Best for | Network blip, brief timeout | Dependency genuinely down |
| Risk if used alone | Amplifies load on a dying service | Does not recover from transient blips |

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.retry.annotation.Retry;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class PaymentClient {

    @Retry(name = "payment", fallbackMethod = "fallbackPayment")
    public String processPayment(String accountId) {
        // Retry: tries 3 times with exponential backoff
        // Good for: network blip, momentary 503
        // Bad for: payment service genuinely down for 10 minutes
        return paymentService.charge(accountId);
    }

    public String fallbackPayment(String accountId, Throwable t) {
        return "Payment queued for retry";
    }
}
```

```yaml
resilience4j:
  retry:
    instances:
      payment:
        maxAttempts: 3
        waitDuration: 1s
        enableExponentialBackoff: true
        exponentialBackoffMultiplier: 2
        retryExceptions:
          - java.io.IOException
          - java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException
```

```text
Retry behavior:
  Attempt 1: fails (IOException)
  Wait 1s
  Attempt 2: fails (IOException)
  Wait 2s
  Attempt 3: succeeds -> return result
  OR Attempt 3: fails -> fallback
```

### What is the default aspect-order gotcha?

By default, Retry is outermost wrapping CircuitBreaker. When the circuit is OPEN, the breaker throws CallNotPermittedException. Retry sees this as just another failure and retries, defeating the purpose of failing fast.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class AspectOrderGotcha {
    // DEFAULT ORDER: Retry -> CircuitBreaker -> actual call
    //
    // Circuit is OPEN:
    //   Attempt 1: CircuitBreaker throws CallNotPermittedException
    //   Retry sees failure -> waits 1s
    //   Attempt 2: CircuitBreaker throws CallNotPermittedException (still open!)
    //   Retry sees failure -> waits 2s
    //   Attempt 3: CircuitBreaker throws CallNotPermittedException
    //   -> 3 wasted retries, 3 seconds lost, OPEN circuit was already saying "stop"

    // CORRECT ORDER: CircuitBreaker -> Retry -> actual call
    //   Circuit is OPEN:
    //     CircuitBreaker throws CallNotPermittedException immediately
    //     NO retries, fails fast in 1ms
    //   Circuit is CLOSED:
    //     Call goes through Retry -> actual call
    //     If transient failure, Retry handles it inside the closed circuit

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("DEFAULT (WRONG): Retry wraps CircuitBreaker");
        System.out.println("  Open circuit -> Retry retries CallNotPermittedException 3x");
        System.out.println("  Wastes 3 seconds, defeats fail-fast purpose");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: CircuitBreaker wraps Retry");
        System.out.println("  Open circuit -> fails fast immediately, 0 retries");
        System.out.println("  Closed circuit -> Retry handles transient failures inside");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
DEFAULT (WRONG): Retry wraps CircuitBreaker
  Open circuit -> Retry retries CallNotPermittedException 3x
  Wastes 3 seconds, defeats fail-fast purpose

CORRECT: CircuitBreaker wraps Retry
  Open circuit -> fails fast immediately, 0 retries
  Closed circuit -> Retry handles transient failures inside
```

### How do you fix the aspect order?

Configure the aspect order so CircuitBreaker is outermost, or tell Retry to ignore CallNotPermittedException.

```yaml
# Fix 1: Reorder aspects so CircuitBreaker is outermost
resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    circuitBreakerAspectOrder: 1
  retry:
    retryAspectOrder: 2
  # Lower number = outermost. CircuitBreaker (1) wraps Retry (2)
```

```yaml
# Fix 2: Tell Retry to ignore CallNotPermittedException
resilience4j:
  retry:
    instances:
      payment:
        ignoreExceptions:
          - io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CallNotPermittedException
```

```text
Fix 1 behavior:
  Open circuit -> CircuitBreaker rejects immediately -> Retry never sees it

Fix 2 behavior:
  Open circuit -> Retry sees CallNotPermittedException -> skips retry -> fallback
```

### Why must you pair a circuit breaker with a timeout?

A circuit breaker cannot trip on calls that never return. Without a timeout, a hanging call holds a thread forever and the breaker never sees a failure to count.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class TimeoutPairing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: CircuitBreaker without timeout");
        System.out.println("  Call hangs for 5 minutes -> thread blocked");
        System.out.println("  Breaker sees no failure (call hasn't returned)");
        System.out.println("  Breaker stays CLOSED -> more threads hang -> cascade");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: Timeout + CircuitBreaker");
        System.out.println("  Call exceeds 3s timeout -> TimeoutException thrown");
        System.out.println("  Breaker counts it as a failure");
        System.out.println("  After threshold -> circuit opens -> calls fail fast");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: CircuitBreaker without timeout
  Call hangs for 5 minutes -> thread blocked
  Breaker sees no failure (call hasn't returned)
  Breaker stays CLOSED -> more threads hang -> cascade

CORRECT: Timeout + CircuitBreaker
  Call exceeds 3s timeout -> TimeoutException thrown
  Breaker counts it as a failure
  After threshold -> circuit opens -> calls fail fast
```

### How do you use it with a fallback?

The fallback returns a safe default when the circuit is open or the call fails.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class InterestRateClient {

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "interestRate", fallbackMethod = "defaultRate")
    public BigDecimal getCurrentRate(String productId) {
        return externalRateService.getRate(productId);
    }

    public BigDecimal defaultRate(String productId, Throwable t) {
        // Return last known rate from cache, not a guess
        System.out.println("Using cached rate for " + productId);
        return new BigDecimal("7.50");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (circuit open):
Using cached rate for HOME-LOAN-30Y
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Expose the circuit state as a metric (Prometheus gauge), size the window and thresholds to traffic volume, combine with a bulkhead, and use slowCallRateThreshold to catch unhealthy-but-up dependencies.

```text
Senior checklist:
  [x] Circuit state exposed as Prometheus metric
  [x] Aspect order: CircuitBreaker outermost
  [x] Timeout paired with every circuit breaker
  [x] Fallback is genuinely safe (cached value, not another risky call)
  [x] slowCallRateThreshold catches slow-but-up dependencies
  [x] Bulkhead paired to isolate thread pools
  [x] Window and thresholds tuned to traffic volume
```
